export const pgUnit09CourseFMarkdown: LocalizedText = {
  "en": "# PG Unit 9 Course F: Construction & Small-World Play\n\n## Course Overview\n\n### Course Type\n\nConstruction & Small-World Play\n\n### Course Purpose\n\nThis course supports PG children's early construction, spatial awareness, object placement, hand-eye coordination, purposeful use of building materials, and small-world participation through concrete summer-themed building experiences. Children build, place, carry, stack, connect, knock down safely, arrange, and return materials with adult support.\n\nUnit 9 uses summer, water, picnic, beach, and garden contexts as building prompts. The focus is not to create complex structures or complete teacher-designed models. Instead, summer settings give children meaningful reasons to build shade spaces, water paths, picnic places, beach worlds, and garden areas using large wooden blocks, interlocking bricks, and selected natural materials.\n\nPG children may participate by placing one block, connecting a few bricks, carrying a material, adding a figure, moving a toy through the structure, or helping put materials back. The main evidence is safe construction participation, spatial exploration, material choice, and willingness to join shared small-world play.\n\n---\n\n# Lesson 1: Big Block Shade House\n\n## Lesson Outcome\n\n### 1. Cognitive Objectives\n\nChildren begin to understand that blocks can be used to make a simple space for a puppet, toy, or child-sized summer rest area.\n\n### 2. Skill-based Objectives\n\nChildren practise carrying, placing, stacking, lining up, and adjusting large wooden blocks with teacher support.\n\n### 3. Affective Objectives\n\nChildren build confidence and group belonging by helping create a shared shade house for a summer puppet or toy.\n\n## HighScope KDI Alignment\n\n### Domain: A. Approaches to Learning\n\n**Items:** 3. Engagement; 4. Problem solving; 5. Use of resources\n\n**How We Support:** Children engage with large wooden blocks as purposeful building resources. They try simple placement actions, notice when a block needs to move, and help create a usable summer shade structure with adult guidance.\n\n### Domain: C. Physical Development and Health\n\n**Items:** 16. Gross-motor skills; 17. Fine-motor skills; 18. Body awareness\n\n**How We Support:** Children use whole-body movement to carry and place large blocks safely, while also using hands to align, adjust, and steady materials. The teacher supports safe spacing, lifting, and movement around the structure.\n\n## Theme Story Context\n\nA puppet or teddy has been playing in the sun and needs a shady place to rest. The teacher invites children to help build a small shade house using large wooden blocks, soft blocks, cloth, and a mat.\n\nChildren may place blocks around a mat, help make a short wall, add a cloth roof with teacher support, or bring Teddy into the finished shade house. The structure does not need to be complex or fully enclosed. The main focus is large-material construction and shared building participation.\n\n## Light Theme Language\n\n```text\nsun\nshade\nhouse\nblock\nbuild\nbig\nput here\nrest\n```\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n```text\nCarry slowly.\nBlock here.\nPut it down.\nBuild together.\nBig block.\nTeddy needs shade.\nCareful body.\nAll done.\nGood building.\n```\n\n## Suggested Activities / Games\n\n### 1. Teddy's Shade House\n\nChildren use large wooden blocks and a soft mat to build a simple shade house for Teddy. The teacher may add a cloth roof while children help hold or place the blocks.\n\n### 2. Block Wall Helpers\n\nChildren work with the teacher to make a short block wall. Each child adds one large block, then steps back to see the wall grow.\n\n### 3. Shade House Visit\n\nAfter the structure is built, children take turns placing Teddy, a hat, a cup, or a towel inside the shade house, turning the build into a small-world care scene.\n\n---\n\n# Lesson 2: Interlocking Brick Water Path\n\n## Lesson Outcome\n\n### 1. Cognitive Objectives\n\nChildren begin to understand that small building pieces can be connected or placed to make a simple path, bridge, or water play setting for small toys.\n\n### 2. Skill-based Objectives\n\nChildren practise connecting interlocking bricks, placing pieces in a line, adding small figures, and moving a boat, duck, or fish toy through a simple constructed path with adult support.\n\n### 3. Affective Objectives\n\nChildren build curiosity and persistence when exploring small construction materials in a playful water-themed small world.\n\n## HighScope KDI Alignment\n\n### Domain: A. Approaches to Learning\n\n**Items:** 3. Engagement; 4. Problem solving; 5. Use of resources\n\n**How We Support:** Children engage with interlocking bricks as flexible construction resources. They explore how pieces connect, how a path can continue, and how small toys can move through or along their construction.\n\n### Domain: C. Physical Development and Health\n\n**Items:** 17. Fine-motor skills; 18. Body awareness\n\n**How We Support:** Children use fingers and hands to connect, press, place, and remove interlocking bricks. The teacher supports safe posture, manageable piece size, and careful use of small construction materials.\n\n## Theme Story Context\n\nThe teacher sets up a blue cloth, tray, or paper river as a simple water world. Small ducks, boats, or fish need a path, bridge, or resting place. Children use interlocking bricks to build along the water.\n\nChildren may connect a few bricks, place bricks beside the water, add a small bridge, or move a boat through the path. The goal is not a detailed model. The focus is fine-motor construction, spatial placement, and small-world engagement.\n\n## Light Theme Language\n\n```text\nwater\nboat\nduck\nfish\nbrick\npath\nbridge\ngo\nstop\n```\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n```text\nPress down.\nClick.\nPut it here.\nMake a path.\nBoat goes.\nDuck here.\nOne more brick.\nCareful hands.\nGood building.\n```\n\n## Suggested Activities / Games\n\n### 1. Blue River Brick Path\n\nChildren place interlocking bricks along a blue cloth or paper river to make a simple water path for ducks, fish, or boats.\n\n### 2. Tiny Bridge Builder\n\nThe teacher models a very simple bridge using larger interlocking bricks. Children add one or two pieces, then help a small boat or duck go under or beside the bridge.\n\n### 3. Duck Stop Stations\n\nChildren build small brick \"stations\" along the water path. The duck toy travels from one station to the next with teacher narration.\n\n---\n\n# Lesson 3: Picnic Place Builders\n\n## Lesson Outcome\n\n### 1. Cognitive Objectives\n\nChildren begin to understand that construction materials can be used to make a simple pretend picnic space with places for food, cups, friends, and baskets.\n\n### 2. Skill-based Objectives\n\nChildren practise arranging blocks, interlocking bricks, cups, plates, baskets, cloth, and small figures to create a picnic setting with adult support.\n\n### 3. Affective Objectives\n\nChildren experience shared building as a warm group activity and begin to feel proud of contributing to a picnic small world.\n\n## HighScope KDI Alignment\n\n### Domain: A. Approaches to Learning\n\n**Items:** 3. Engagement; 5. Use of resources\n\n**How We Support:** Children engage with construction and pretend-play materials as resources for making a picnic setting. They use blocks, cloth, baskets, and small props purposefully with teacher support.\n\n### Domain: B. Social and Emotional Development\n\n**Items:** 11. Community; 13. Cooperative play\n\n**How We Support:** Children contribute to a shared picnic build by adding one item, placing a prop, or helping a toy friend sit at the picnic. The teacher supports early cooperative participation without expecting complex group planning.\n\n## Theme Story Context\n\nThe picnic friends need a place to sit, eat, and share. The teacher invites children to build a picnic place using a mat, low blocks, interlocking brick pieces, baskets, pretend fruit, cups, plates, and small animal figures.\n\nChildren may make a picnic table with blocks, place cups on a mat, build a small fence around the picnic area, or add animal friends. The focus is arranging and constructing a small-world setting, not table manners or fruit vocabulary.\n\n## Light Theme Language\n\n```text\npicnic\nmat\nbasket\nfruit\ncup\nfriend\ntable\nsit\nbuild\n```\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n```text\nPicnic here.\nPut the mat here.\nBuild a table.\nCup here.\nFruit here.\nFriend sits here.\nOne more.\nClean up.\nGood building.\n```\n\n## Suggested Activities / Games\n\n### 1. Block Picnic Table\n\nChildren use large wooden blocks or soft blocks to create a low picnic table or sitting area, then place pretend fruit and cups on top.\n\n### 2. Brick Fruit Stand\n\nChildren use interlocking bricks to build a tiny fruit stand or basket area. Pretend fruits or color blocks can be placed inside.\n\n### 3. Animal Picnic Small World\n\nChildren add animal figures, cups, plates, and baskets to the picnic build, then help the animals sit, eat pretend food, and return items after play.\n\n---\n\n# Lesson 4: Beach and Garden Small World\n\n## Lesson Outcome\n\n### 1. Cognitive Objectives\n\nChildren begin to understand that mixed materials can be arranged to create a simple beach or garden small world.\n\n### 2. Skill-based Objectives\n\nChildren practise placing, lining up, stacking, connecting, scooping, arranging, and returning materials such as interlocking bricks, blocks, stones, leaves, sticks, shells, buckets, and small figures with adult support.\n\n### 3. Affective Objectives\n\nChildren build curiosity, care, and confidence as they help create and tidy a shared summer small-world scene.\n\n## HighScope KDI Alignment\n\n### Domain: A. Approaches to Learning\n\n**Items:** 3. Engagement; 4. Problem solving; 5. Use of resources\n\n**How We Support:** Children engage with a mix of construction and natural materials to create a beach or garden scene. They explore where materials can go, how small parts can be arranged, and how to return resources to their homes after play.\n\n### Domain: G. Science and Technology\n\n**Items:** 45. Observing; 51. Natural and physical world\n\n**How We Support:** Children observe and handle natural materials such as smooth stones, leaves, sticks, shells, and sand-like textures within a constructed small world. The teacher supports sensory noticing and safe handling without turning the lesson into formal science instruction.\n\n## Theme Story Context\n\nThe teacher prepares two simple small-world zones: a beach area and a garden area. Children use interlocking bricks, large or medium blocks, smooth stones, leaves, sticks, shells, small buckets, scoops, and toy figures to create a summer world.\n\nChildren may place stones as a path, use interlocking bricks as a small wall, add leaves to the garden, place shells on the beach, or move a toy figure through the scene. The teacher keeps materials safe, limited, and manageable for PG children.\n\nThe focus is mixed-material construction and small-world participation. Children are not expected to design a complete scene independently.\n\n## Light Theme Language\n\n```text\nbeach\ngarden\nsand\nstone\nshell\nleaf\nstick\nbucket\nbuild\nput back\n```\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n```text\nBeach here.\nGarden here.\nStone path.\nLeaf here.\nBuild with bricks.\nPut the shell here.\nKeep it in the tray.\nPut it back.\nGood building.\n```\n\n## Suggested Activities / Games\n\n### 1. Stone Path to the Beach\n\nChildren place smooth stones or large safe pebbles in a short path leading to a beach tray. A small figure or toy walks along the path with teacher support.\n\n### 2. Brick Garden Wall\n\nChildren use interlocking bricks to build a short garden wall or flower bed edge, then add leaves, sticks, or pretend plants inside.\n\n### 3. Summer World Basket Return\n\nAfter play, children help return materials to their homes using clear basket cues: bricks in one basket, stones in one tray, leaves and sticks in another, and small figures in a toy basket.\n",
  "zh": "# PG Unit 9 Course F: 建构与小世界游戏\n\n## 课程概览\n\n### 课程类型\n\n建构与小世界游戏\n\n### 课程目的\n\n本课程通过具体的夏季主题建构体验，支持 PG 儿童的早期建构、空间意识、物品摆放、手眼协调、有目的地使用建构材料，以及小世界参与。儿童在成人支持下进行搭建、放置、搬运、堆叠、连接、安全推倒、排列和归还材料。\n\nUnit 9 使用夏天、水、野餐、海滩和花园情境作为建构提示。重点不是创造复杂结构或完成教师设计的模型，而是让儿童用大木块、拼插积木和选定自然材料，围绕遮阴空间、水路径、野餐地点、海滩世界和花园区域进行有意义的搭建。\n\nPG 儿童可以放置一块积木、连接几块砖、搬一个材料、添加一个小人物、让玩具穿过结构，或帮助把材料放回去。主要证据是安全建构参与、空间探索、材料选择和愿意加入共享小世界游戏。\n\n---\n\n# 第一课: 大积木遮阴屋\n\n## 课程结果\n\n### 1. 认知目标\n\n儿童开始理解积木可以用来为偶偶、玩具或儿童大小的夏季休息区域做一个简单空间。\n\n### 2. 技能目标\n\n儿童在教师支持下练习搬运、放置、堆叠、排成一排和调整大木块。\n\n### 3. 情感目标\n\n儿童通过帮助为夏季偶偶或玩具搭建共享遮阴屋，建立信心和小组归属感。\n\n## HighScope KDI 对齐\n\n### Domain: A. Approaches to Learning\n\n**Items:** 3. Engagement; 4. Problem solving; 5. Use of resources\n\n**支持方式:** 儿童把大木块作为有目的的建构资源参与活动。他们尝试简单放置动作，注意什么时候需要移动积木，并在成人引导下帮助搭建可用的夏季遮阴结构。\n\n### Domain: C. Physical Development and Health\n\n**Items:** 16. Gross-motor skills; 17. Fine-motor skills; 18. Body awareness\n\n**支持方式:** 儿童用全身动作安全搬运和放置大积木，也用手对齐、调整和稳定材料。教师支持结构周围的安全间距、搬运和移动。\n\n## 主题故事情境\n\n偶偶或 Teddy 在太阳下玩过，需要一个阴凉地方休息。教师邀请儿童用大木块、软积木、布和垫子帮助搭建小遮阴屋。\n\n儿童可以把积木放在垫子周围、帮助做一段短墙、在教师支持下添加布屋顶，或把 Teddy 带进完成的遮阴屋。结构不需要复杂或完全封闭。主要重点是大材料建构和共享搭建参与。\n\n## Light Theme Language\n\n```text\nsun\nshade\nhouse\nblock\nbuild\nbig\nput here\nrest\n```\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n```text\nCarry slowly.\nBlock here.\nPut it down.\nBuild together.\nBig block.\nTeddy needs shade.\nCareful body.\nAll done.\nGood building.\n```\n\n## 建议活动 / 游戏\n\n### 1. Teddy's Shade House\n\n儿童用大木块和软垫为 Teddy 搭建简单遮阴屋。教师可以添加布屋顶，儿童帮助扶住或放置积木。\n\n### 2. Block Wall Helpers\n\n儿童和教师一起做一段短积木墙。每位儿童添加一块大积木，然后退后看墙慢慢长高。\n\n### 3. Shade House Visit\n\n结构完成后，儿童轮流把 Teddy、帽子、杯子或毛巾放进遮阴屋，把建构变成小世界照料场景。\n\n---\n\n# 第二课: 拼插积木水路径\n\n## 课程结果\n\n### 1. 认知目标\n\n儿童开始理解小建构材料可以连接或摆放成简单路径、桥或给小玩具使用的水游戏场景。\n\n### 2. 技能目标\n\n儿童在成人支持下练习连接拼插积木、把积木排成线、添加小人物，并让小船、小鸭或小鱼玩具通过简单建构路径。\n\n### 3. 情感目标\n\n儿童在有游戏感的水主题小世界中探索小建构材料，建立好奇和坚持。\n\n## HighScope KDI 对齐\n\n### Domain: A. Approaches to Learning\n\n**Items:** 3. Engagement; 4. Problem solving; 5. Use of resources\n\n**支持方式:** 儿童把拼插积木作为灵活建构资源使用。他们探索积木如何连接、路径如何延伸，以及小玩具如何穿过或沿着建构移动。\n\n### Domain: C. Physical Development and Health\n\n**Items:** 17. Fine-motor skills; 18. Body awareness\n\n**支持方式:** 儿童用手指和手连接、按压、放置和拆下拼插积木。教师支持安全姿势、适当大小的积木和小建构材料的谨慎使用。\n\n## 主题故事情境\n\n教师设置蓝布、托盘或纸河作为简单水世界。小鸭、小船或小鱼需要一条路径、桥或休息处。儿童用拼插积木沿着水边搭建。\n\n儿童可以连接几块积木、把积木放在水边、添加小桥，或让小船通过路径。目标不是详细模型，而是精细动作建构、空间放置和小世界参与。\n\n## Light Theme Language\n\n```text\nwater\nboat\nduck\nfish\nbrick\npath\nbridge\ngo\nstop\n```\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n```text\nPress down.\nClick.\nPut it here.\nMake a path.\nBoat goes.\nDuck here.\nOne more brick.\nCareful hands.\nGood building.\n```\n\n## 建议活动 / 游戏\n\n### 1. Blue River Brick Path\n\n儿童沿着蓝布或纸河摆放拼插积木，为小鸭、小鱼或小船做简单水路径。\n\n### 2. Tiny Bridge Builder\n\n教师用较大的拼插积木示范非常简单的桥。儿童添加一两块，然后帮助小船或小鸭从桥下或旁边经过。\n\n### 3. Duck Stop Stations\n\n儿童沿水路径搭建小小 “stations”。教师叙述，鸭子玩具从一个站点移动到下一个站点。\n\n---\n\n# 第三课: 野餐地点建构者\n\n## 课程结果\n\n### 1. 认知目标\n\n儿童开始理解建构材料可以用来做一个简单假想野餐空间，包含食物、杯子、朋友和篮子的位置。\n\n### 2. 技能目标\n\n儿童在成人支持下练习排列积木、拼插积木、杯子、盘子、篮子、布和小人物，创建野餐场景。\n\n### 3. 情感目标\n\n儿童把共享建构体验为温暖的小组活动，并开始为自己对野餐小世界的贡献感到自豪。\n\n## HighScope KDI 对齐\n\n### Domain: A. Approaches to Learning\n\n**Items:** 3. Engagement; 5. Use of resources\n\n**支持方式:** 儿童把建构和假想游戏材料作为制作野餐场景的资源，在教师支持下有目的地使用积木、布、篮子和小道具。\n\n### Domain: B. Social and Emotional Development\n\n**Items:** 11. Community; 13. Cooperative play\n\n**支持方式:** 儿童通过添加一个物品、放置一个道具，或帮助玩具朋友坐到野餐处，为共享野餐建构做贡献。教师支持早期合作参与，不期待复杂小组计划。\n\n## 主题故事情境\n\n野餐朋友需要一个坐下、吃东西和分享的地方。教师邀请儿童用垫子、低矮积木、拼插积木、篮子、假想水果、杯子、盘子和小动物人物搭建野餐地点。\n\n儿童可以用积木做野餐桌、把杯子放在垫子上、在野餐区域周围搭小围栏，或添加动物朋友。重点是排列和建构小世界场景，不是餐桌礼仪或水果词汇。\n\n## Light Theme Language\n\n```text\npicnic\nmat\nbasket\nfruit\ncup\nfriend\ntable\nsit\nbuild\n```\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n```text\nPicnic here.\nPut the mat here.\nBuild a table.\nCup here.\nFruit here.\nFriend sits here.\nOne more.\nClean up.\nGood building.\n```\n\n## 建议活动 / 游戏\n\n### 1. Block Picnic Table\n\n儿童用大木块或软积木制作低矮野餐桌或座位区域，然后把假想水果和杯子放在上面。\n\n### 2. Brick Fruit Stand\n\n儿童用拼插积木搭建小水果摊或篮子区域。假想水果或颜色积木可以放进去。\n\n### 3. Animal Picnic Small World\n\n儿童把动物人物、杯子、盘子和篮子添加到野餐建构中，然后帮助动物坐下、假装吃东西，并在游戏后归还物品。\n\n---\n\n# 第四课: 海滩与花园小世界\n\n## 课程结果\n\n### 1. 认知目标\n\n儿童开始理解混合材料可以被排列成简单的海滩或花园小世界。\n\n### 2. 技能目标\n\n儿童在成人支持下练习放置、排成线、堆叠、连接、舀、排列和归还拼插积木、积木、石头、树叶、树枝、贝壳、小桶和小人物。\n\n### 3. 情感目标\n\n儿童在帮助创建和整理共享夏季小世界场景中建立好奇、照料和信心。\n\n## HighScope KDI 对齐\n\n### Domain: A. Approaches to Learning\n\n**Items:** 3. Engagement; 4. Problem solving; 5. Use of resources\n\n**支持方式:** 儿童使用建构材料和自然材料混合创建海滩或花园场景。他们探索材料可以放在哪里、小部件如何排列，以及游戏后如何把资源送回家。\n\n### Domain: G. Science and Technology\n\n**Items:** 45. Observing; 51. Natural and physical world\n\n**支持方式:** 儿童在建构小世界中观察并操作光滑石头、树叶、树枝、贝壳和沙感材料。教师支持感官注意和安全操作，而不把课程变成正式科学教学。\n\n## 主题故事情境\n\n教师准备两个简单小世界区域：海滩区和花园区。儿童使用拼插积木、大或中型积木、光滑石头、树叶、树枝、贝壳、小桶、舀勺和玩具人物创建夏季世界。\n\n儿童可以把石头放成路径、用拼插积木做小墙、把树叶加到花园中、把贝壳放到海滩上，或让玩具人物穿过场景。教师让材料保持安全、有限且适合 PG 儿童操作。\n\n重点是混合材料建构和小世界参与。儿童不需要独立设计完整场景。\n\n## Light Theme Language\n\n```text\nbeach\ngarden\nsand\nstone\nshell\nleaf\nstick\nbucket\nbuild\nput back\n```\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n```text\nBeach here.\nGarden here.\nStone path.\nLeaf here.\nBuild with bricks.\nPut the shell here.\nKeep it in the tray.\nPut it back.\nGood building.\n```\n\n## 建议活动 / 游戏\n\n### 1. Stone Path to the Beach\n\n儿童把光滑石头或大型安全鹅卵石放成一条通向海滩托盘的短路径。小人物或玩具在教师支持下沿路径行走。\n\n### 2. Brick Garden Wall\n\n儿童用拼插积木搭建一段短花园墙或花坛边缘，然后在里面添加树叶、树枝或假想植物。\n\n### 3. Summer World Basket Return\n\n游戏后，儿童根据清晰篮子提示帮助材料回家：积木放一个篮子，石头放一个托盘，树叶和树枝放另一个，小人物放玩具篮。\n"
};

export const pgUnit09CourseGMarkdown: LocalizedText = {
  "en": "# PG Unit 9 Course G: PSED & Safety\n\n## Course Overview\n\n### Course Type\n\nPSED & Safety\n\n### Course Purpose\n\nThis course supports PG children's early personal, social, emotional development and safety awareness through concrete summer routines, shared play situations, and adult-supported safety experiences. Children practise waiting briefly, taking turns, using gentle hands, staying near the teacher, moving safely, keeping safe body space, and returning to calm routines after outdoor or water-related play.\n\nUnit 9 uses summer, water, picnic, beach, and garden contexts as story settings for social-emotional learning and safety routines. The focus is not to teach safety rules through explanation or require children to verbalize feelings. Instead, children experience safety and care through repeated actions, visible cues, teacher modelling, and predictable group routines.\n\nPG children may participate by looking, pausing, waiting briefly, giving an item, holding a teacher's hand, moving slowly, stopping on cue, staying on a mat, using gentle touch, or accepting comfort. The main evidence is supported participation in safe routines, not independent rule mastery.\n\n---\n\n# Lesson 1: Stay Cool, Stay Near\n\n## Lesson Outcome\n\n### 1. Cognitive Objectives\n\nChildren begin to understand that during summer outdoor play, staying near the teacher and going to shade or water together helps the group feel safe and cared for.\n\n### 2. Skill-based Objectives\n\nChildren practise staying close to the group, following a teacher gesture, moving toward shade, drinking water with support, and pausing when the teacher gives a stop cue.\n\n### 3. Affective Objectives\n\nChildren build emotional security during warm-weather transitions by experiencing shade, water, and teacher closeness as predictable supports.\n\n## HighScope KDI Alignment\n\n### Domain: B. Social and Emotional Development\n\n**Items:** 8. Sense of competence; 11. Community; 12. Building relationships\n\n**How We Support:** Children experience themselves as part of a safe group routine by staying near the teacher, moving with peers, and receiving care during hot-weather moments. Adult closeness and predictable cues support relationship-building and a sense of competence.\n\n### Domain: C. Physical Development and Health\n\n**Items:** 18. Body awareness; 20. Healthy behavior\n\n**How We Support:** Children are supported to notice basic body-care routines such as drinking water, resting in shade, and slowing down after active play. These actions support body awareness and early healthy behavior without requiring verbal explanation.\n\n## Theme Story Context\n\nThe teacher creates a short \"summer helper group\" story. The class is going outside or pretending to play in the sun, and everyone needs to stay near the teacher, find shade, drink water, and rest together.\n\nA puppet may wander too far from the shade mat and need help coming back. Children help by pointing, moving with the teacher, or bringing the puppet to the group. The focus is staying near, following cues, and feeling safe in a summer routine.\n\n## Light Theme Language\n\n```text\nsun\nshade\nwater\nhot\ncool\nnear\nstop\ncome here\n```\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n```text\nStay near me.\nCome here.\nThis way.\nStop.\nFind shade.\nDrink water.\nRest here.\nGood staying close.\n```\n\n## Suggested Activities / Games\n\n### 1. Shade Mat Home\n\nThe teacher places a shade mat or blue cloth as the group's safe home. Children practise moving away a few steps, then coming back to the shade mat when the teacher calls.\n\n### 2. Teddy Comes Back\n\nTeddy moves too far from the group. Children help call, point, or bring Teddy back to the teacher and the shade area.\n\n### 3. Stop and Sip\n\nChildren walk slowly with the teacher. When the teacher says `Stop`, everyone pauses, then pretends to drink water or uses their own water bottle routine with support.\n\n---\n\n# Lesson 2: Gentle Hands at the Water Station\n\n## Lesson Outcome\n\n### 1. Cognitive Objectives\n\nChildren begin to understand that water tools, towels, and toys should be touched gently and used one at a time with teacher support.\n\n### 2. Skill-based Objectives\n\nChildren practise waiting briefly, taking one turn, using gentle hands, passing or returning a water tool, and keeping materials inside the tray.\n\n### 3. Affective Objectives\n\nChildren begin to feel secure during shared water play by experiencing clear turns, predictable teacher support, and calm group boundaries.\n\n## HighScope KDI Alignment\n\n### Domain: B. Social and Emotional Development\n\n**Items:** 9. Emotions; 11. Community; 13. Cooperative play\n\n**How We Support:** Children are supported through the emotional challenge of waiting and sharing highly attractive water materials. Teacher modelling, short turns, and visual routines help children participate as part of a group without needing to regulate independently.\n\n### Domain: A. Approaches to Learning\n\n**Items:** 3. Engagement; 5. Use of resources\n\n**How We Support:** Children engage with water tools, cups, towels, and toys while learning that shared materials can be used safely and returned. The teacher supports purposeful use of resources within clear boundaries.\n\n## Theme Story Context\n\nThe teacher sets up a small water station where ducks, boats, cups, and towels are waiting for careful helpers. The water toys like gentle hands and calm short turns. Each child can have a short supported turn with one tool or toy.\n\nThe teacher uses strong visual structure: one tray, one tool, one turn, then put it back. Children may touch water, scoop once, dry a toy, or return a cup. The main focus is safe shared participation, not water exploration itself.\n\n## Light Theme Language\n\n```text\nwater\nwet\ndry\ncup\ntowel\nduck\nturn\ngentle hands\n```\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n```text\nWait.\nYour turn.\nGentle hands.\nOne cup.\nKeep it in the tray.\nPut it back.\nThank you.\nGood waiting.\n```\n\n## Suggested Activities / Games\n\n### 1. One Cup Turn\n\nEach child gets one short turn with one cup at the water tray. The teacher supports scoop, pour, and put back before the next child's turn.\n\n### 2. Gentle Duck Bath\n\nChildren gently wash or pat a duck toy, then place it on a towel. The duck only likes soft hands.\n\n### 3. Towel Pass Calm Turn\n\nA small towel becomes the turn marker. The child holding the towel has a turn, then gives the towel back to the teacher or passes it with support.\n\n---\n\n# Lesson 3: Picnic Sharing and Waiting\n\n## Lesson Outcome\n\n### 1. Cognitive Objectives\n\nChildren begin to understand that picnic play involves shared items, simple waiting, giving, receiving, and cleaning up together.\n\n### 2. Skill-based Objectives\n\nChildren practise giving a fruit, receiving a cup or napkin, waiting briefly for a turn, sitting near the group, and placing used items in a basket.\n\n### 3. Affective Objectives\n\nChildren build a sense of belonging and emotional comfort through a warm shared picnic routine where each child can contribute.\n\n## HighScope KDI Alignment\n\n### Domain: B. Social and Emotional Development\n\n**Items:** 11. Community; 12. Building relationships; 13. Cooperative play\n\n**How We Support:** Children participate in a group picnic routine by sitting near others, giving and receiving items, and helping clean up. The teacher supports early relationship-building and cooperative play through concrete, repeated actions.\n\n### Domain: A. Approaches to Learning\n\n**Items:** 3. Engagement; 5. Use of resources\n\n**How We Support:** Children engage with picnic props such as fruit, cups, napkins, mats, and baskets. They use these resources in simple social routines with adult guidance.\n\n## Theme Story Context\n\nThe teacher creates a small picnic scene with a mat, basket, pretend fruits, cups, napkins, and puppet friends. Everyone wants to join the picnic, but the group needs to wait, share, pass items, and clean up together.\n\nChildren may give one fruit to a puppet, receive a napkin, sit near the picnic mat, or place a used item into the basket. The picnic theme makes waiting and sharing concrete and emotionally warm.\n\n## Light Theme Language\n\n```text\npicnic\nfruit\ncup\nnapkin\nfriend\nwait\nshare\nbasket\n```\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n```text\nSit here.\nWait.\nYour turn.\nGive it to Teddy.\nThank you.\nShare.\nPut it in the basket.\nGood picnic helper.\n```\n\n## Suggested Activities / Games\n\n### 1. Fruit for a Friend\n\nChildren take turns giving one pretend fruit to a puppet or peer with teacher support. The turn is short and predictable.\n\n### 2. Picnic Pass Basket\n\nA small basket moves around the group. Each child places one picnic item inside, then gives the basket back to the teacher or next child.\n\n### 3. Napkin Helper Circle\n\nChildren help give napkins to puppet friends or classmates. The teacher supports waiting, giving, and saying thank you through gestures or simple teacher language.\n\n---\n\n# Lesson 4: Safe Body at the Beach and Garden\n\n## Lesson Outcome\n\n### 1. Cognitive Objectives\n\nChildren begin to understand that beach and garden play needs safe bodies, safe space, gentle hands, and slow movement.\n\n### 2. Skill-based Objectives\n\nChildren practise walking slowly, stopping on cue, keeping materials in a tray, touching natural objects gently, and returning tools or toys after play.\n\n### 3. Affective Objectives\n\nChildren begin to experience safety routines as supportive and predictable, helping them feel secure during active summer play.\n\n## HighScope KDI Alignment\n\n### Domain: C. Physical Development and Health\n\n**Items:** 16. Gross-motor skills; 18. Body awareness; 20. Healthy behavior\n\n**How We Support:** Children practise safe movement, stopping, slow walking, and body-space awareness around beach and garden materials. The teacher supports healthy and safe play habits through repeated action cues.\n\n### Domain: B. Social and Emotional Development\n\n**Items:** 9. Emotions; 11. Community\n\n**How We Support:** Children are supported to regulate excitement during active play by using stop cues, calm spaces, and shared clean-up routines. They participate as part of a group that keeps the play area safe together.\n\n## Theme Story Context\n\nThe teacher creates a simple beach and garden safety path. The beach has buckets, towels, shells, and sand-like materials. The garden has leaves, stones, sticks, and toy plants. The materials need gentle hands, and children need safe bodies.\n\nChildren practise moving slowly through the space, stopping at markers, touching one item gently, and returning it to the tray. The focus is safe body control and safe material use, not beach or garden knowledge.\n\n## Light Theme Language\n\n```text\nbeach\ngarden\nsand\nstone\nleaf\nbucket\nsafe body\ngentle hands\nstop\n```\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n```text\nSafe body.\nWalk slowly.\nStop.\nGentle hands.\nKeep it in the tray.\nPut it back.\nThis way.\nAll done.\nGood safe body.\n```\n\n## Suggested Activities / Games\n\n### 1. Beach Path Stop-and-Go\n\nChildren walk slowly along a short beach path made with mats or floor markers. The teacher gives simple `go` and `stop` cues.\n\n### 2. Gentle Garden Touch\n\nChildren touch one leaf, stone, stick, or pretend plant gently, then place it back into the garden tray.\n\n### 3. Safe Body Clean-Up\n\nChildren return buckets, shells, stones, leaves, and tools to baskets while practising slow walking and safe body space.\n",
  "zh": "# PG Unit 9 Course G: PSED 与安全\n\n## 课程概览\n\n### 课程类型\n\nPSED 与安全\n\n### 课程目的\n\n本课程通过具体夏季常规、共享游戏情境和成人支持的安全体验，支持 PG 儿童早期个人、社会、情感发展和安全意识。儿童练习短暂等待、轮流、使用温柔小手、靠近教师、安全移动、保持安全身体空间，以及在户外或水相关游戏后回到安定常规。\n\nUnit 9 使用夏天、水、野餐、海滩和花园情境作为社会情感学习和安全常规的故事设置。重点不是通过解释来教授安全规则，也不是要求儿童口头表达感受。儿童通过重复动作、可见提示、教师示范和可预测的小组常规体验安全和照料。\n\nPG 儿童可以通过看、停顿、短暂等待、递物品、牵教师的手、慢慢移动、听提示停止、留在垫子上、温柔触摸或接受安抚来参与。主要证据是被支持地参与安全常规，而不是独立掌握规则。\n\n---\n\n# 第一课: 保持凉爽，靠近小组\n\n## 课程结果\n\n### 1. 认知目标\n\n儿童开始理解在夏季户外游戏中，靠近教师，并一起去遮阴处或饮水处，可以帮助小组感到安全和被照料。\n\n### 2. 技能目标\n\n儿童练习靠近小组、跟随教师手势、移动到遮阴处、在支持下喝水，以及在教师给出 stop 提示时暂停。\n\n### 3. 情感目标\n\n儿童通过把遮阴、水和教师靠近体验为可预测支持，在温暖天气转换中建立情绪安全感。\n\n## HighScope KDI 对齐\n\n### Domain: B. Social and Emotional Development\n\n**Items:** 8. Sense of competence; 11. Community; 12. Building relationships\n\n**支持方式:** 儿童通过靠近教师、与同伴一起移动，并在热天时刻接受照料，体验自己是安全小组常规的一部分。成人靠近和可预测提示支持关系建立和能力感。\n\n### Domain: C. Physical Development and Health\n\n**Items:** 18. Body awareness; 20. Healthy behavior\n\n**支持方式:** 儿童被支持去注意基本身体照料常规，例如喝水、在阴凉处休息和活动后放慢速度。这些动作支持身体意识和早期健康行为，不要求口头解释。\n\n## 主题故事情境\n\n教师创建一个短小的 “summer helper group” 故事。班级要去户外或假装在太阳下玩，大家需要靠近教师、找遮阴处、喝水并一起休息。\n\n偶偶可能离遮阴垫太远，需要帮助回到小组。儿童通过指、跟教师移动或把偶偶带回小组来帮忙。重点是靠近、跟随提示，并在夏季常规中感到安全。\n\n## Light Theme Language\n\n```text\nsun\nshade\nwater\nhot\ncool\nnear\nstop\ncome here\n```\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n```text\nStay near me.\nCome here.\nThis way.\nStop.\nFind shade.\nDrink water.\nRest here.\nGood staying close.\n```\n\n## 建议活动 / 游戏\n\n### 1. Shade Mat Home\n\n教师把遮阴垫或蓝布作为小组的安全家。儿童练习离开几步，然后在教师呼唤时回到遮阴垫。\n\n### 2. Teddy Comes Back\n\nTeddy 离小组太远。儿童帮助呼唤、指向，或把 Teddy 带回教师和遮阴区域。\n\n### 3. Stop and Sip\n\n儿童和教师一起慢慢走。当教师说 `Stop` 时，大家暂停，然后假装喝水或在支持下使用自己的水瓶常规。\n\n---\n\n# 第二课: 水站里的温柔小手\n\n## 课程结果\n\n### 1. 认知目标\n\n儿童开始理解水工具、毛巾和玩具应被轻轻触摸，并在教师支持下一次使用一个。\n\n### 2. 技能目标\n\n儿童练习短暂等待、轮到自己一次、使用温柔小手、传递或归还水工具，并把材料留在托盘内。\n\n### 3. 情感目标\n\n儿童通过清晰轮流、可预测教师支持和安静小组边界，在共享水游戏中感到安全。\n\n## HighScope KDI 对齐\n\n### Domain: B. Social and Emotional Development\n\n**Items:** 9. Emotions; 11. Community; 13. Cooperative play\n\n**支持方式:** 儿童在等待和分享非常有吸引力的水材料时获得支持。教师示范、短轮次和视觉常规帮助儿童作为小组一员参与，而不需要独立完成情绪调节。\n\n### Domain: A. Approaches to Learning\n\n**Items:** 3. Engagement; 5. Use of resources\n\n**支持方式:** 儿童接触水工具、杯子、毛巾和玩具，同时学习共享材料可以被安全使用和归还。教师在清晰边界中支持资源的有目的使用。\n\n## 主题故事情境\n\n教师设置一个小水站，小鸭、小船、杯子和毛巾在等待细心帮手。水玩具喜欢温柔小手和安静短轮次。每位儿童可以用一个工具或玩具有一个短暂支持轮次。\n\n教师使用强视觉结构：一个托盘、一个工具、一次轮次，然后放回去。儿童可以触水、舀一次、擦干一个玩具或归还一个杯子。主要重点是安全共享参与，而不是水探索本身。\n\n## Light Theme Language\n\n```text\nwater\nwet\ndry\ncup\ntowel\nduck\nturn\ngentle hands\n```\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n```text\nWait.\nYour turn.\nGentle hands.\nOne cup.\nKeep it in the tray.\nPut it back.\nThank you.\nGood waiting.\n```\n\n## 建议活动 / 游戏\n\n### 1. One Cup Turn\n\n每位儿童在水托盘前用一个杯子进行一个短轮次。教师支持 scoop、pour 和 put back，然后轮到下一位儿童。\n\n### 2. Gentle Duck Bath\n\n儿童轻轻清洗或拍拍鸭子玩具，然后把它放到毛巾上。小鸭只喜欢 soft hands。\n\n### 3. Towel Pass Calm Turn\n\n小毛巾成为轮流标记。拿着毛巾的儿童进行一次轮次，然后在支持下把毛巾交回教师或传给别人。\n\n---\n\n# 第三课: 野餐分享与等待\n\n## 课程结果\n\n### 1. 认知目标\n\n儿童开始理解野餐游戏包含共享物品、简单等待、给予、接收和一起收拾。\n\n### 2. 技能目标\n\n儿童练习给一个水果、接收杯子或餐巾、短暂等待轮到自己、坐在小组附近，并把用过的物品放进篮子。\n\n### 3. 情感目标\n\n儿童通过温暖的共享野餐常规建立归属感和情绪舒适感，每个儿童都可以做出贡献。\n\n## HighScope KDI 对齐\n\n### Domain: B. Social and Emotional Development\n\n**Items:** 11. Community; 12. Building relationships; 13. Cooperative play\n\n**支持方式:** 儿童通过坐在他人附近、给予和接收物品、帮助收拾，参与小组野餐常规。教师通过具体重复动作支持早期关系建立和合作游戏。\n\n### Domain: A. Approaches to Learning\n\n**Items:** 3. Engagement; 5. Use of resources\n\n**支持方式:** 儿童接触水果、杯子、餐巾、垫子和篮子等野餐道具，并在成人引导下把这些资源用于简单社会性常规。\n\n## 主题故事情境\n\n教师用垫子、篮子、假想水果、杯子、餐巾和偶偶朋友创建一个小野餐场景。每个人都想加入野餐，但小组需要等待、分享、传递物品并一起收拾。\n\n儿童可以给偶偶一个水果、接收餐巾、坐在野餐垫旁，或把用过的物品放进篮子。野餐主题让等待和分享变得具体而温暖。\n\n## Light Theme Language\n\n```text\npicnic\nfruit\ncup\nnapkin\nfriend\nwait\nshare\nbasket\n```\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n```text\nSit here.\nWait.\nYour turn.\nGive it to Teddy.\nThank you.\nShare.\nPut it in the basket.\nGood picnic helper.\n```\n\n## 建议活动 / 游戏\n\n### 1. Fruit for a Friend\n\n儿童在教师支持下轮流把一个假想水果给偶偶或同伴。轮次短而可预测。\n\n### 2. Picnic Pass Basket\n\n小篮子在小组中移动。每位儿童放入一个野餐物品，然后把篮子交回教师或传给下一位儿童。\n\n### 3. Napkin Helper Circle\n\n儿童帮助给偶偶朋友或同伴递餐巾。教师通过手势或简单教师语言支持等待、给予和 saying thank you。\n\n---\n\n# 第四课: 海滩与花园里的安全身体\n\n## 课程结果\n\n### 1. 认知目标\n\n儿童开始理解海滩和花园游戏需要安全身体、安全空间、温柔小手和慢慢移动。\n\n### 2. 技能目标\n\n儿童练习慢慢走、听提示停止、把材料留在托盘内、轻轻触摸自然物品，以及游戏后归还工具或玩具。\n\n### 3. 情感目标\n\n儿童开始把安全常规体验为支持性和可预测的内容，帮助他们在活跃夏季游戏中感到安全。\n\n## HighScope KDI 对齐\n\n### Domain: C. Physical Development and Health\n\n**Items:** 16. Gross-motor skills; 18. Body awareness; 20. Healthy behavior\n\n**支持方式:** 儿童在海滩和花园材料周围练习安全移动、停止、慢走和身体空间意识。教师通过重复动作提示支持健康和安全游戏习惯。\n\n### Domain: B. Social and Emotional Development\n\n**Items:** 9. Emotions; 11. Community\n\n**支持方式:** 儿童通过 stop 提示、安静空间和共享收拾常规，在活跃游戏中获得调节兴奋的支持。他们作为小组一员参与，共同保持游戏区域安全。\n\n## 主题故事情境\n\n教师创建一条简单海滩和花园安全路径。海滩有小桶、毛巾、贝壳和沙感材料。花园有树叶、石头、树枝和玩具植物。材料需要温柔小手，儿童需要安全身体。\n\n儿童练习慢慢穿过空间、在标记处停止、轻轻触摸一个物品，并把它放回托盘。重点是安全身体控制和安全材料使用，不是海滩或花园知识。\n\n## Light Theme Language\n\n```text\nbeach\ngarden\nsand\nstone\nleaf\nbucket\nsafe body\ngentle hands\nstop\n```\n\n## Teacher Routine Language\n\n```text\nSafe body.\nWalk slowly.\nStop.\nGentle hands.\nKeep it in the tray.\nPut it back.\nThis way.\nAll done.\nGood safe body.\n```\n\n## 建议活动 / 游戏\n\n### 1. Beach Path Stop-and-Go\n\n儿童沿着由垫子或地面标记组成的短海滩路径慢慢走。教师给出简单 `go` 和 `stop` 提示。\n\n### 2. Gentle Garden Touch\n\n儿童轻轻触摸一片树叶、一块石头、一根树枝或一个假想植物，然后把它放回花园托盘。\n\n### 3. Safe Body Clean-Up\n\n儿童归还小桶、贝壳、石头、树叶和工具到篮子中，同时练习慢走和安全身体空间。\n"
};
